messages.content:
I have a tokio-based HTTP server written in Rust. Under high load the server occasionally deadlocks — all worker threads are stuck and no new requests are processed. I added tracing instrumentation and I can see that the last events before the deadlock are always a burst of concurrent inference requests hitting the prompt-cache lookup path. The cache itself is protected by a tokio::sync::Mutex. Here is a simplified version of the hot path:

```rust
async fn handle_inference(state: Arc<AppState>, req: InferRequest) -> Response {
    let mut cache = state.prompt_cache.lock().await;
    let cached = cache.lookup(&req.prompt_hash);
    if let Some(kv) = cached {
        drop(cache);
        return run_decode_with_cache(state.clone(), req, kv).await;
    }
    // Cache miss: run full prefill while holding the lock.
    let kv = run_prefill(state.clone(), &req).await;
    cache.insert(req.prompt_hash.clone(), kv.clone());
    run_decode_with_cache(state.clone(), req, kv).await
}
```

The run_prefill function takes 500ms–2,000ms per call and internally calls state.model.forward() which is a blocking CPU/GPU operation wrapped in spawn_blocking. What is the deadlock cause and how do I fix it?

The deadlock is caused by holding the tokio::sync::Mutex across an await point that itself needs to acquire the same executor resources your lock is blocking. Here is the precise failure chain:

1. Request A acquires the prompt_cache lock on worker thread W1 and reaches the cache miss branch. It calls run_prefill(...).await, which internally calls spawn_blocking to run the GPU forward pass. The .await suspends the current task and releases W1 back to the tokio thread pool.

2. While Request A is suspended but still holding the lock, requests B, C, D, ... arrive and each tries to acquire the same prompt_cache lock via lock().await. They all block at that await point. Their futures are parked in the tokio task queue, waiting for the lock to become available. Each parked future occupies one pending task in the executor.

3. The spawn_blocking call from run_prefill needs a blocking thread from tokio's blocking thread pool (separate from the async worker pool). If the blocking thread pool is saturated, the spawn_blocking future itself cannot make progress. But the spawn_blocking future can only complete and unpark Request A if a worker thread is available to drive it — and all worker threads may be occupied driving the parked lock-waiters (B, C, D, ...) in a spin.

4. The true deadlock crystallizes when all async worker threads are parked on lock().await and no thread is free to poll the spawn_blocking completion future. The blocking thread pool completes its work but nobody is awake to notice.

The fix has two parts:

Part 1 — Never hold an async Mutex across a long await. Use a synchronous std::sync::Mutex for the cache lookup (which is fast — a hash map probe), drop it before any async operation:

```rust
async fn handle_inference(state: Arc<AppState>, req: InferRequest) -> Response {
    // Fast path: check cache under a synchronous lock, drop immediately.
    let cached = {
        let cache = state.prompt_cache.lock().unwrap(); // std::sync::Mutex
        cache.lookup(&req.prompt_hash).cloned()
    }; // lock released here

    if let Some(kv) = cached {
        return run_decode_with_cache(state.clone(), req, kv).await;
    }

    // Slow path: run prefill without any lock held.
    let kv = run_prefill(state.clone(), &req).await;

    // Re-acquire for insertion. Use try_insert to handle the race where
    // another task completed the same prefill concurrently.
    {
        let mut cache = state.prompt_cache.lock().unwrap();
        cache.entry(req.prompt_hash.clone()).or_insert(kv.clone());
    }

    run_decode_with_cache(state.clone(), req, kv).await
}
```

Part 2 — Accept the thundering herd on a cache miss and deduplicate with a per-key flight tracker if needed. The or_insert pattern above is safe but may run redundant prefills. If that is expensive, use a DashMap<PromptHash, Arc<Notify>> to coalesce waiters on the same key.

The rule: std::sync::Mutex is correct for fast critical sections even in async code. tokio::sync::Mutex is only needed when you genuinely must hold a lock across an await — which is almost never the right design.
